const intro={"n":6,"title":"Desertion, Homecoming, and the Loss of Family","summary":"After hearing plans for another military expedition, Fierro decides that remaining at the frontier may destroy him. One night, while the commander and justice of the peace are drinking and playing cards, he escapes on horseback. He returns to his home district after three years, but finds his rancho reduced to ruins. A neighbor tells him his livestock has been sold and his children have gone to work. His wife has left, apparently in search of food and survival. Fierro blesses his children from afar, grieves his family, and resolves to live as a fugitive gaucho.","people":"Martín Fierro; the absent wife and children he recalls; a neighbor who provides news; the commanding officer and justice of the peace mentioned before the escape.","themes":"Desertion and freedom; the destruction of home; grief and paternal responsibility; poverty and displacement; the beginning of the outlaw's identity.","words":"desertor/resertor (deserter, represented in gauchesque speech); pago (home district); tapera (ruined or abandoned dwelling); matrero (fugitive/outlaw gaucho); gauchaje (gaucho community); toldería (Indigenous settlement, in the army's planned expedition).","questions":["Which events immediately precede Fierro's escape, and what reasons does he give for leaving?","What does Fierro know firsthand on returning, and what does the neighbor tell him?","How does he speak about his wife's departure? Is his account accusatory or sympathetic?","Why does the ruined home matter for the shift from working gaucho to matrero?","What is the connection between his blessing to the children and his later advice in the 1879 sequel?"],"nums":[165,169,178],"analysis":"This canto closes the gap between the earlier image of a settled rural worker and the man who calls himself a fugitive. It is tempting to summarize the wife as having simply 'abandoned' the family, but Fierro himself relates that extreme hunger may have driven her away. His later moral advice to his sons gains weight from this separation. The poem's narration remains one man's perspective, even when the suffering is moving.","related":"/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/","relatedTitle":"Fierro's advice to his sons"};
